From what I understand about copyright law, the main burden of a prosecutor
in copyright cases is to prove that the infringement actually resulted in lost
money due to this added competition (ie: people bought the infringing product
rather than the original one largely because of the duplication).

Example 1: Kinko's Copies, a nationwide photocopy chain was successfully sued
by a publisher's association because of their lax copying policies wherein
students were copying large parts of textbooks instead of purchasing them.
Kinko's had to pay millions in damages.
                                         
Example 2: A professor at my University took excerpts of music videos from
MTV (without permission from them), and created a documentary supposedly 
showing how music videos promoted rape.  He then started selling his
documentary on videotape.  It put MTV in a very bad light.  MTV sued him for 
copyright violation for using the videos without permission.  I believe
the case ended up being thrown out or dropped, because MTV couldn't show
that he had cost them profits because he used their videos in a manner that
was competitive with their own use of the videos.  

Anyway, I don't think that the small uses of Tolkien, Moorcock, etc references
could be seen as competitive or damaging to the author's works, so I don't
think NetHack has too much to worry about... 

